////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Acid Test: Instantiation Body Binders
//
// A declaration's propositional atom is text#bodySignature, and the
// signature renders the body's bound variables via their canonical
// alpha-equivalence ProperNames (x₁, x₂, ...).  When a declaration with a
// body is instantiated as a side effect of matching some other Weeny - as a
// typed Let with an environment body must be, since it is a forbidden
// Weeny - the EF value substituted into its body arrives carrying the
// ProperNames of the user proposition it was copied from.  If that
// proposition nested the binder one level deeper (here under ∃!), the
// binder is named x₂ there but x₁ in the user's Let body.
//
// Regression: insertInstantiation assigned ProperNames before renaming the
// instantiation's bindings, so the instantiated Let got a different atom
// (t#...x₂...) from the user's identical Let (t#...x₁...) and the
// Let-environment premise was never discharged.  The bindings must be
// renamed first.
//
// Each section below uses its own disjoint constants so that rules and
// instantiations cannot cross-justify between sections.

➤ "typed ∃! introduction, environment-body Let, quantified property"
{
  Declare ∃!, ∀, c, Q, S
  Rule: :{ :t in S :@P(t)  :{ Let y in S be such that @P(y)  y=t }  ∃!x in S.@P(x) }
  Rule: :{ :∀a.Q(a,y)   y=c }
  Assume ∀a.Q(a,c)
  Assume c in S
  { Let t in S be such that ∀a.Q(a,t)
    t=c ✔︎
  }
  ∃!x in S.∀a.Q(a,x) ✔︎
}

➤ "same, with an implication under the quantifier mentioning an outer Let"
{
  Declare ∃!, ∀, ⇒, d, R, T, f, g
  Rule: :{ :t in T :@P(t)  :{ Let y in T be such that @P(y)  y=t }  ∃!x in T.@P(x) }
  Rule: :{ :∀a.(B=g(a) ⇒ y=f(a))   y=d }
  Let B
  Assume ∀a.(B=g(a) ⇒ d=f(a))
  Assume d in T
  { Let t in T be such that ∀a.(B=g(a) ⇒ t=f(a))
    t=d ✔︎
  }
  ∃!x in T.∀a.(B=g(a) ⇒ x=f(a)) ✔︎
}

➤ "untyped expression-body Let still works"
{
  Declare ∃!, ∀, e, W
  Rule: :{ :@P(t)  :{ Let y be such that @P(y)  y=t }  ∃!x.@P(x) }
  Rule: :{ :∀a.W(a,y)   y=e }
  Assume ∀a.W(a,e)
  { Let t be such that ∀a.W(a,t)
    t=e ✔︎
  }
  ∃!x.∀a.W(a,x) ✔︎
}
